WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:04.399
Ah, the Game of Life.

00:00:04.399 --> 00:00:09.580
It’s about as offensive as a bowl of Jello.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.710
But the original one from a hundred years
earlier?

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:25.010
It had squares like...this.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.880
The first Game of Life wasn’t just a game.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.510
It was a form of moral instruction.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:39.200
And it says something about how society thinks
life should be lived then and now.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:47.510
In a way, the Game of Life started when this
chin disappeared.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:52.819
Milton Bradley was a young lithographer — basically
a printer — in Massachusetts when he made

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:57.539
a thousand prints of this man running
for president in 1860.

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:00.959
When Abraham Lincoln grew a beard, those prints
were worthless.

00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:02.480
So Bradley had to pivot.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.109
He took his printing skills and let them loose
on a young medium: board games.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:10.530
The Checkered Game of Life was his first game
— and it became a hit.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.030
Players started at infancy.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:17.090
They spun a teetotum — this thing — to
determine options for their move.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.159
You had control to choose your
move once you spun.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:25.560
The goal was to hit 100 points, through 5-point milestones like college, and Congress,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.450
or big ones, like 50 points for Old Age.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:33.270
The game’s patent shows that Milton
Bradley’s Life was more than just a social

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.270
game.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:40.649
It was about great moral principles.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:46.310
Elizabeth Peabody founded the first English
kindergarten in the United States in 1860.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.500
Milton Bradley published this portrait of
her well after his Lincoln failure.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.140
He also volunteered to teach his own daughter’s
kindergarten class in Springfield, Massachusetts,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.460
after the success of Life.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.710
And he used his business, Milton Bradley and
Company, to publish games and educational

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.880
tools, including more than 40 books about
the new Kindergarten curriculum.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.069
They made a wide variety of learning tools,
from educational puzzles to influential color

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:15.450
wheels.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.010
Education became Bradley’s passion, and
the original Game of Life predicted that — it

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:26.370
was a way to teach “the checkered journey
of life” to children — and adults.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.810
That weird spinner, the teetotum?

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:33.069
That was originally to avoid cards and dice,
because they were associated with gambling.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.319
The location of each spot also taught a lesson.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:39.510
Old age was surrounded by many difficulties.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.180
“Poverty lies near the cradle,” but passing
through it didn’t hurt you in the beginning

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:45.180
of the game.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:54.120
Setbacks didn't earn you points, but most
didn't kick you out of the game, either.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.080
Honesty led to happiness.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.870
Industry, to wealth.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.860
And perseverance led to success.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.319
“I made 50,000 in the stock market today.”

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:14.019
“That’s Life”
In 1960, long after Milton Bradley died, the

00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:19.430
company — which by then was mostly making
games — dug Life from the archives, choosing

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:24.720
it over a long list of other games the company
had once published.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.459
They adapted it to 1960s America with a candy-colored
spinner and stacks of cash and cars that could

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:37.050
load up a full family of baby boomers to places
like Millionaire Acres.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.040
“I went to the Poor Farm.”

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.030
“I’m on Millionaire Acres!”

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:48.190
It centered around paydays, where the value
of winning a Nobel was the cash prize that came

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:49.540
with it.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.920
The winner is the person with the most money.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:58.410
Today’s versions are almost identical, with
tweaks for jobs and hot brand integrations.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.560
“That’s life.”

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:08.609
There was no more disgrace but there also
wasn’t bravery, or honor, or truth.

00:04:08.609 --> 00:04:12.349
Both versions are the Game of Life.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:15.300
Which one should we play?

